body:
MoE iteration spaces could retain a Loopy routing name after collision avoidance renamed the corresponding Tron routing field, causing generated C++ to use a stale identifier. This change resolves routing references during Loopy-to-Tron lowering so generated loops use the routing field actually declared.

## How a tensor name breaks routing references

Loading a tensor from the safetensors key `routing` reserves that Tron name. A Loopy routing also named `routing` is then declared as `routing_1`, but its iteration space still carries `routing`. Previously, C++ generation emitted that original name directly.

Illustrative generated C++ for this collision:

```cpp
// Before: references the original name instead of the declared routing.
routing->active_experts();
routing->per_expert[expert];

// After: references the declared routing field.
routing_1->active_experts();
routing_1->per_expert[expert];
```

## Resolve routing references at the lowering boundary

Tron's `IterationSpace` now carries names typed as `Name ('Tron 'MoeRouting)`. `lowerIterSpace` obtains those names from `stateEnv`, which records the routing field allocated for each Loopy variable. The existing iteration-space traversal applies that lookup to buffer, channel, and kernel iteration spaces, including routing references embedded in finite size expressions. C++ generation consequently receives the resolved routing name.

Nested routing loops also had an existing lowering failure: dependency analysis collected routing names from loop types, but `kernelEnvBinding` rejected `RoutingInfo`. This PR accepts those references as iteration-space metadata while keeping routing objects out of ordinary kernel expressions.

Kernel bodies consume resolved module bindings through `KernelEnv` and manage their own lexical scope. The existing kernel dependency analysis includes routing names from nested loop types, so `kernelEnvBinding` places those bindings in a separate routing map alongside the expression bindings. Nested loops resolve routing references through that map; numeric bounds resolve through expression bindings in the current lexical scope, where enclosing loop indices and configuration values are available.

The prerequisite PRs provide the separate routing/scalar parameters, channel traversal, and numeric-bound resolution. This layer uses that groundwork to resolve routing names.

## Validation

The regression test constructs a scope-valid MoE module with the tensor/routing collision above. It verifies that both `active_experts()` and `per_expert[expert]` use the routing name recorded in Tron declarations, and that a nested loop's lowered iteration space references that same declaration. The collision assertions fail without routing-name resolution; the nested-loop case also catches rejection of routing references during kernel environment preparation.

Generated MoE body kernels distribute activation work with nested expert/row loops and per-expert offset arithmetic. Use the router's existing `expert_major` vector to express the same worker assignment with one strided loop.

[#3895](https://github.com/positron-ai/tron/pull/3895) fixed helper imbalance by continuing row-to-helper assignment across expert boundaries: previously, every expert's first row went to helper 0. It implemented this global assignment with a running row count and modulo arithmetic inside the nested traversal. The router already exposed the desired row sequence in `expert_major` when that PR landed, so this change simplifies its implementation while retaining its balancing behavior.

With tracing, channel operations, and kernel arithmetic omitted, the generated traversal changes from:

```cpp
size_t n_prior_rows = 0;
for (const expert_id expert : routing->active_experts()) {
  const size_t n_rows = routing->per_expert[expert].size();
  const size_t first_row_ix =
    (actual_worker_ix + n_actual_workers - n_prior_rows % n_actual_workers)
    % n_actual_workers;
  for (size_t row_ix = first_row_ix; row_ix < n_rows; row_ix += n_actual_workers) {
    auto [token, slot] = routing->expert_row(expert, expert_row_id(row_ix));
    token_slot_id<4> ix(token, slot);
    // Process ix.
  }
  n_prior_rows += n_rows;
}
```

to:

```cpp
for (size_t row_ix = actual_worker_ix;
     row_ix < routing->expert_major.size();
     row_ix += n_actual_workers) {
  token_slot_id<4> ix = routing->expert_major[row_ix];
  // Process ix.
}
```

The constructor builds `expert_major` by concatenating the same experts' rows in the same order. Both loops assign global row `r` to helper `r % n_actual_workers`, preserving the existing balance across expert boundaries. Main-thread exclusion, single-worker behavior, trace placement, per-row channel waits/writes, and kernel arithmetic stay unchanged.

The accompanying comments clarify that expert-major order means grouping by ascending expert ID; matmul dispatch and completion can occur in a different order.

The existing MoE code-generation test checks the flat loop's worker offset, full row bound, stride, and token-slot lookup.

## Summary

Llama's non-permuted WQ launch now calls `xs_norm_channel.start_reading(0)` before its completion callback calls `finish_reading(0)`, so WQ enters each `xs_norm` phase through the consumer barrier like every other participant.

## Problem

`xs_norm_channel` is reset with three consumer participants: WQ is 0, WK is 1 and WV is 2 (`reset(n_main_workers, 3)`). WK and WV each call `start_reading` in `launch_kv_jobs` and `finish_reading` in their callbacks. `launch_wq` calls `start_reading` only for its permuted input, `xs_q_perm_channel`. In non-permuted builds, where `xs_norm` is WQ's input, the callback calls `xs_norm_channel.finish_reading(0)` with no matching `start_reading(0)`.

The two calls do different jobs:

- `start_reading` is the consumer's phase gate. Its `consumer_barrier.wait` returns once `prepare` has started this phase, and `prepare` re-arms the per-item latches that the WQ jobs wait on.
- `finish_reading` records the participant's completed phase and counts it down, so the next `prepare` can go ahead.

Because the done count still balances, nothing deadlocks. WQ, though, never passes the gate, so nothing orders its launch after this phase's `prepare`.

Nothing breaks today. `prepare_xs_norm` runs before `launch_wq` on every path, both at layer 0 and in the layer loop. The risk is inferred, not reproduced: if a later change moved that `prepare` after the launch, WQ's jobs could see the previous layer's ready latches and read stale `xs_norm` items.

## Change

Add the missing call in the non-permuted branch, mirroring the permuted one:

```cpp
if constexpr (permuted) {
  q_batch->xs_q_perm_channel.start_reading(0);
} else {
  q_batch->xs_norm_channel.start_reading(0);
}
```

`xs_norm` is a per-item channel (`tensor_latch<signaled_producer_t>`), so `start_reading` passes only the phase barrier and never waits for data. Since `prepare` has already run, it returns at once, and WQ's schedule is unchanged.

## Summary

This deletes the `SingleHostP` channel producer, which nothing has constructed since the MoE blend became token-parallel. This is a behavior-preserving refactor.

## Why

`SingleHostP` marked a buffer that the main thread alone writes. Its channel reset with one producer and used the whole-phase fallback latch. Its only user was the non-parallel MoE blend. The main thread accumulated every token while helpers ran the downstream consumers, and the channel made those helpers wait for the finished result (the deleted Note [SingleHostP Producer]).

e93cd2a0fb made `MoeBlend` token-parallel, and liveness has recorded its writes as `ParP` ever since. Since then nothing on `main` records a `SingleHostP` write; every remaining mention is a case arm or a comment. Every consumer of `Producer` still had to handle the role, though:

- reset counts;
- the channel's latch choice;
- `BufferReuse`'s sync scheduling;
- TronCheck's scope and drain checks;
- liveness's output rule.

Anyone reading those tables had to reason about a role that can't occur. Deleting the constructor removes those arms, the Note, and the comments that listed it. All 18 generated plugins regenerate byte-identically.

## Note formatting

Note [Channel execution domains] also loses its `SingleHostP` mention. fourmolu treats `-- *` as a Haddock heading, so it had separated each bullet's first line from the rest of the bullet with a blank line. The bullets now use `--   -`, which fourmolu leaves intact; their wording is otherwise unchanged.
